course: history
lesson: 77
title: What was lost and what was gained: languages, foods, music and knowledge
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When two rivers meet, the water mixes into something new, but some fish from each river are lost; when peoples met, they too made new things, like jazz, Haitian Creole, mestizo culture and fish and chips, and lost others, like burned books and languages that nearly fell silent.
explain: In Europa Universalis a province «converts» or «changes culture» with a click and the old one is simply gone; in real life old languages and customs often survived quietly in grandparents' kitchens, and some have come back, like Māori in New Zealand and Hebrew as an everyday language.
explain: Knowledge flowed both ways: Andean peoples knew that cinchona bark helped with fever, which gave the world quinine against malaria, and maize, potatoes and rubber came from Indigenous farmers.
explain: Some questions are still argued today, like who should keep the Parthenon marbles, and some have been settled by respect, like closing the climb on Uluru.
explain: History is not about blaming children today; it is about understanding what was lost and gained, and treating each other fairly now.
fact: About 7,000 languages are spoken today, and around 4 in 10 are endangered; Māori language nests, kōhanga reo, began in 1982, and Hebrew was revived as an everyday language around 1900.
fact: Welsh, once discouraged in schools, is now taught in every school in Wales, and the Welsh government aims for a million speakers by 2050.
fact: Meetings of peoples created new peoples, cultures and languages, like mestizo culture in Mexico and creole languages such as Haitian Creole, which grew from French and African languages.
fact: Jazz and blues grew among African American communities, mixing African rhythms with European instruments; reggae and hip-hop also came from such mixing.
fact: Fried fish came to Britain with Jewish immigrants from Portugal and Spain; Japanese tempura grew from Portuguese cooking in the 1500s; where chicken tikka masala was first made is argued between Britain and India.
fact: Fewer than 20 books from before the Spanish conquest survive from Mexico and Central America, because many were burned by Spanish priests or lost.
fact: Quinine, from the bark of the cinchona tree, was known to Andean peoples and became the main medicine for malaria for centuries; maize, potatoes and rubber also came from Indigenous peoples of the Americas.
fact: Indigenous populations fell in many places after contact but are growing again; about 1 in 6 New Zealanders is Māori.
fact: Lord Elgin's agents took marbles from the Parthenon in Athens in the early 1800s; Greece wants them back, the British Museum says it owns them legally, and it is still debated.
fact: Uluru in Australia, once called Ayers Rock, is sacred to the Anangu people, and climbing it was banned in 2019 to respect them.
fact: Children today are not to blame for what empires did long ago; history helps people understand each other and act fairly now.
exercise: About how many languages are spoken in the world today? | About 7,000 | About 70 | About 700,000 | About 7
exercise: What is a kōhanga reo? | A Māori language nest for young children | A Māori fishing boat carved from a single tree | A New Zealand rugby team for young players | A kind of Māori bread
exercise: Which language was revived as an everyday language around 1900? | Hebrew | Latin, in Rome | Ancient Egyptian | Sumerian, in Iraq
exercise: What is Haitian Creole? | A new language that grew from French and African languages | A dialect of English brought to Haiti by sailors from Jamaica | A secret code once spoken only by pirates | A language invented in 2000
exercise: What does «mestizo» describe in Mexico? | A culture mixing Indigenous and Spanish roots | A kind of Aztec temple built on top of a hill | A Spanish army that marched on Mexico City | A type of cactus
exercise: Where did jazz grow up? | Among African American communities | In the royal courts and palaces of Europe | In the tea houses of ancient China | In Viking villages
exercise: Who brought fried fish to Britain? | Jewish immigrants from Portugal and Spain | Roman soldiers guarding Hadrian's Wall | Viking raiders from Denmark and Norway | Chinese sailors
exercise: Where did Japanese tempura come from? | Portuguese cooking in the 1500s | Ancient Egypt, by the Silk Road | American fast food after the war | Russian soldiers
exercise: Why do so few books from before the Spanish conquest of Mexico survive? | Many were burned by Spanish priests or lost | The Aztecs never wrote anything | They were all sold to China | They were written on ice
exercise: What disease was quinine used against? | Malaria | Chickenpox | Toothache | The common cold
exercise: Where did the knowledge of cinchona bark come from? | Andean peoples of South America | Doctors working in London hospitals | Monks living in Tibet | Sailors from Norway
exercise: About how many New Zealanders are Māori? | About 1 in 6 | About 1 in 1,000 | Nearly all of them | None at all today
exercise: Who wants the Parthenon marbles returned? | Greece | Egypt, for Cairo | Norway, for Oslo | Japan, for a museum
exercise: Why was climbing Uluru banned in 2019? | It is sacred to the Anangu people | It became too slippery after the rains | It was sold to a big company | Tourists were all bored of climbing it
exercise: Is a child today to blame for what an empire did long ago? | No, history is for understanding, not blaming children | Yes, every child is to blame | Only children with old surnames | Only if they play strategy games
test: Wiremu: "My nan went to kōhanga reo, a language nest. Te reo Māori was nearly lost, chur." ("Chur" means cheers, yeah, in NZ slang.) What does this show? | A language that nearly died can come back | Lost languages can never return once they start to fade | Māori was never in any danger at all | Language nests are for birds only, chur
test: In Europa Universalis, Kai's missionary converts a province and the old religion vanishes in one click. What did real history often look like? | Old languages and customs often survived quietly at home, and some came back | Old ways always vanished in a single day, just as they do in the game, with nothing left | Missionaries never went anywhere | Nobody ever changed religion
test: Lucía: "We're a mix of peoples, innit." Her mate from Haiti: "Same, we speak Kreyòl, sak pase!" ("Sak pase" means what's up, in Haitian Creole.) What's the big idea? | Meetings of peoples created new cultures and languages | Every culture is pure and has never been mixed with any other | Creole languages are just broken French with mistakes | Mixing always destroys everything
test: Jaden's grandad: "Jazz, that's real cool, daddy-o." ("Daddy-o" is old slang for mate.) Where did jazz come from? | African American communities, mixing African rhythms and European instruments | Royal orchestras in Vienna, who played it for the emperor and his court at night | Ancient Greek theatres | A computer program
test: Callum: "Fish and chips is 100% British, nothing foreign in it, mate." His mate: "Nah, the fish came with Jewish families from Portugal." Who's right? | The mate: fried fish came with Jewish immigrants from Portugal and Spain | Callum: it was invented by the Queen herself and served at the palace on Fridays | Neither: it came from China | Both: it grew on trees in Britain
test: Mia's cousin: "Aztecs never wrote books, they just did pyramids, mid." ("Mid" means not very good.) What's the correction? | They made books, but fewer than 20 from before the conquest survive | The Aztecs wrote millions of books, and every single one survives today | The Aztecs had no writing of any kind | Aztec books are in every library
test: Ruby's dad: "Quinine? That's in tonic water, innit. Came from Britain." What's more exact? | It came from cinchona bark, known to Andean peoples | It was made in a London lab from nothing | It comes from the sea | It was invented in 2000
test: Ella: "Māori are about 1 in 6 New Zealanders. So they're growing again, sweet as!" ("Sweet as" means great.) Is she right? | Yes, many Indigenous peoples are growing again after falling | No, Māori have disappeared and only a few words of their language are left | No, Māori were never in New Zealand | Yes, but only because of tourists
test: Nikos: "The Parthenon marbles should come home, re!" ("Re" is a Greek call like mate.) His British friend: "They're safe in the museum, though." What's the honest summary? | It is still debated who should keep them | Greece has already got them all back from London | The marbles were made in London | Nobody cares about them anymore
test: Ava's Aussie uncle: "Back in the day we all climbed Ayers Rock, no worries." What changed in 2019? | Climbing Uluru was banned to respect the Anangu people | The rock was moved to Sydney on huge trucks for the tourists | The rock was sold to a company | Climbing was made compulsory for every visitor
test: Oliver, whose family is from a former empire, asks Amara, whose family is from a former colony: "Is it my fault?" Best answer? | No; history helps us understand and treat each other fairly now | Yes, it is entirely his fault | Yes, and Amara must blame him | History has nothing to teach anyone
test: What would have happened if cinchona bark had never left the Andes? | Many more people in the world would have died of malaria for centuries | Malaria would have disappeared on its own, since bark never really helped anyone | Nothing at all would change | Europeans would have invented chocolate
exam: Identify the language which was revived as a language of everyday use at approximately the commencement of the twentieth century. | Hebrew | Latin, in the city of Rome | Sumerian | Ancient Egyptian
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the survival of books from Mexico and Central America produced prior to the Spanish conquest? | Fewer than twenty survive, many having been burned or lost | Several thousand survive intact in the libraries of Mexico City | None were ever produced | All survive in the British Museum
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of quinine. | It derives from the bark of the cinchona tree, known to Andean peoples | It was synthesised in London without any natural source | It derives from seawater | It derives from the leaves of the tea plant
exam: Ascertain the status of the dispute in respect of the Parthenon marbles. | It remains contested between Greece and the British Museum | It was resolved by the full return of the marbles | No party has ever claimed the marbles | The marbles were destroyed in the nineteenth century
